X:1
T:Arirang
T:Korean folk song, as Hulbert wrote it down in 1896
C:traditional (Korea)
O:Korea (Mungyeong Saejae Arirang)
R:Korean minyo
S:Homer B. Hulbert, "Korean Vocal Music", The Korean Repository 3 (Seoul: Trilingual Press, February 1896), p. 51; archive.org, University of Illinois copy
N:The oldest written Arirang, which Homer Hulbert heard everywhere in Seoul in 1896: a fixed chorus that everyone sings ("Arirang, arirang, arariyo") and verses a singer makes up, here one about the birch trees of the Mungyeong Saejae pass, all cut down to make washing clubs. Arirang is a national song of Korea, North and South, with many regional versions; this is the earliest on paper.
N:Hulbert's romanisation is kept (A-ra-rung for Arirang); in today's spelling: "Arirang arirang arariyo, arirang eolsa bae ddiwora. Mungyeong saejae bakdal namu, hongdukkae bangmang-i da naganda." Guessed: bar 2's rhythm (the print shows no flags; read as bar 1, two quavers and a minim). The janggu pattern and open-fifth drone are ours, after the semachi jangdan as commonly taught.
M:3/4
L:1/8
Q:1/4=88
K:G
